\section{Check the Boundary After Signal Updates}

\begin{verbatim}
instantiate choose_word(selected) and choose_word(procedural), WIDTH=12
give both instances a=ABC, b=123 # twelve-bit hexadecimal
for (s, expected) in [('0', ABC), ('1', 123), ('X', 000)]:
    drive sel to both instances
    wait for signal propagation  # do not read y in the drive delta
    assert both outputs == expected
set sel='1'; change only b to 456
wait for signal propagation
assert both outputs == 456       # check data sensitivity, too
\end{verbatim}

In a VHDL testbench a positive wait between drive and check suffices here; the design has no explicit delays. The \verb|'X'| test checks agreement of the RTL models, not a promise that fabricated logic detects ambiguous voltage or metastability. Binary selection is the physical function specified by this small example. Because the two architectures give the same specified result for every \texttt{sel} value in this model, synthesis may produce indistinguishable combinational implementations; architecture names and process boundaries are not required netlist structure.

A width change provides a different test of the boundary. Choose twelve bits and set only the highest bit of the selected input. This detects an accidental eight-bit intermediate that a test with small operands could overlook. If an alternative architecture adds a register, this simple settled-value test is no longer enough: it must also check the cycle in which the word appears, and the architecture may no longer satisfy this combinational interface's contract.
